  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Eternity Symbol (\u221E) represents the idea of something that has
    no finite end or limit. Its continuous shape, made of two connected loops, provides
    a simple visual way to understand concepts such as infinity, continuity, and
    endlessness.\n\nThe idea of eternity is much older than the modern infinity symbol.
    Throughout history, different civilizations used symbols and stories to express
    endless time, recurring cycles, renewal, and eternal existence. For example,
    the Ouroboros, a serpent forming a circle, was associated in ancient traditions
    with cycles, renewal, and eternity. In ancient Indian thought, ideas of cyclical
    time describe recurring processes of creation, preservation, destruction, and
    renewal.\n\nThe modern \u221E symbol, however, has a specific mathematical history.
    In 1655, English mathematician John Wallis used the sideways-eight symbol in
    his mathematical work. Over time, this sign became the widely recognized symbol
    for infinity in mathematics.\n\nThe shape of the symbol is particularly interesting.
    It contains two loops connected at a central intersection. If we imagine a point
    travelling along the path, it can continue moving around one loop, pass through
    the center, enter the other loop, and continue again. There is no natural stopping
    point on the closed path.\n\nIn this 3D and AR experience, the infinity symbol
    becomes an interactive learning object. A glowing point begins the journey and
    leaves a continuous trail behind it. The trail gradually forms the first loop,
    then the second loop, and finally creates the complete infinity shape. Users
    can explore the left loop, intersection, and right loop, rotate the 3D model,
    zoom in and out, change materials, and observe the endless-loop animation.\n\nThe
    Eternity Symbol therefore connects history, mathematics, philosophy, and visual
    learning, helping learners understand how humans have represented the idea of
    endlessness in different ways across time."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
