  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Qutub Minar is one of the most famous historical monuments in Delhi and
    an important example of early Indo-Islamic architecture. Located in the Qutb
    complex at Mehrauli, the monument is known for its impressive height, detailed
    stone carvings, inscriptions, and distinctive architectural design.

    The
    construction of Qutub Minar began in 1199 CE under Qutb-ud-din Aibak. Later,
    Shamsuddin Iltutmish added more storeys to the tower. Some portions were damaged
    over time, and repairs and additions were carried out by later rulers, including
    Firuz Shah Tughlaq.

    The tower is approximately 72.5 metres high. It has
    five storeys, and each storey is separated by a projecting balcony. The balconies
    are supported by beautifully carved brackets and create strong horizontal divisions
    across the tall, tapering structure.

    The lower part of the tower is wider,
    while the diameter gradually decreases as it rises. This gives Qutub Minar its
    elegant and distinctive shape. Its outer surface features different types of
    fluting, including rounded and angular patterns. These grooves create interesting
    patterns of light and shadow across the sandstone.

    Another important feature
    is the series of carved inscription bands found on the tower. The inscriptions
    provide valuable information about the monument''s historical and cultural context.

    Qutub
    Minar is part of the larger Qutb complex, which also contains several other important
    historical structures, including the Quwwat-ul-Islam Mosque, Alai Darwaza, and
    the Iron Pillar.

    In 1993, the Qutb Minar and its Monuments were inscribed
    on the UNESCO World Heritage List. Today, Qutub Minar remains an important symbol
    of India''s architectural heritage and attracts visitors from around the world.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 956b217cc7cf8c3428c64cf5f91c13b5, type: 2}
